.Dd $Mdocdate: September 26 2026 $
.Dt FUGUPASS 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm fugupass
.Nd the limits of a FuguPass vault, and the operator runbook
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A FuguPass vault keeps each entry behind a master plate, a passphrase,
and a set of oracles.
This page states what an attacker gets from each artifact, where the
protection ends, and what the owner and each oracle operator do.
.Xr fugupass 1
holds the commands, the files, and the reports.
.Pp
Five artifacts exist.
The shared set is the ciphertext of the index and of each entry file.
The disk of a machine holds the device factor and the wraps of that
machine.
The passphrase is in the head of the owner.
Each oracle holds the records of the vault and its own static key.
The master plate holds the master, and every other secret derives
from it.
.Pp
Three limits hold in every case.
A compromised machine sees every secret that it reveals while the
compromise lasts.
The oracle is blind: it gates, counts, and wipes, and it does not
verify intent.
The oracle sees the traffic pattern of each record, never the
content.
.Ss What an attacker gets
A copy of the shared set alone gives nothing.
Every file of the set is ciphertext, and the copy holds no device
factor, no wrap, and no verifier.
.Pp
A copy of the shared set plus the passphrase, without the device
factor, gives nothing.
The attacker derives no client key without the device factor, so no
oracle record answers, and the copy holds no wrap to unmask.
.Pp
The disk of a machine, without the passphrase, gives no offline
passphrase attack, because the disk holds no passphrase verifier.
The config file on that disk holds the plate check value.
That value gives an offline test of a candidate master, at the 128
bits of the master.
The wraps add no offline test of their own.
An online guess burns one of the three strikes of one record at one
oracle, and the third wrong guess wipes that record there.
With the default pool of 64 slots and one canary, one machine holds
65 records at each oracle.
Each oracle therefore gives 195 distinct guesses.
The canary seal on the disk confirms a hit at one oracle, so a guess
needs no quorum.
The owner revokes the machine from the plate, and the revocation
section below gives the count of locks.
.Pp
The disk of a machine plus the passphrase is the case that the
oracles cannot see.
Malware in an unlocked session is the same case.
To each oracle, the attacker is the owner.
The attacker reveals entries at its own pace, leaves one log line per
entry at each oracle of its quorum, and burns no strike.
The window closes when the owner reads the logs and revokes the
machine from the plate.
The detection section below gives the bound of that reading.
.Pp
The disk of a machine plus the records of one oracle, with the static
key of that oracle, gives an offline passphrase verifier.
The section on the offline floor below gives that case and the cases
beyond it.
.Pp
The master plate gives everything.
The attacker derives every secret and the keys of every machine, with
no oracle and no passphrase.
Physical custody of the plate is the one countermeasure.
.Pp
The loss of oracle hosts, by seizure, wipe, or failure, costs no
data.
While
.Ar k
oracles of the set stay live and reachable, reveals continue on any
.Ar k
of them, with no ceremony.
With every position live, the set tolerates the loss of
.Ar n No \- Ar k
hosts.
The seized masks alone are meaningless strings, and fewer than
.Ar k
breached oracles unmask nothing.
With fewer than
.Ar k
oracles reachable, reveals pause until a plate ceremony enrolls the
records again at replacement oracles.
.Ss The offline floor
The disk alone holds no passphrase verifier, so no offline passphrase
attack exists against the disk.
The floor of the design sits one breach beyond that claim.
.Pp
The verifier case: an attacker holds the disk of a machine and the
records of one oracle, with its static key.
That attacker searches the passphrase offline.
The record stores the hash of the pin secret of that record, and the
disk holds every salt.
The cost of one guess is one run of
.Xr bcrypt_pbkdf 3
at the round count of the config file.
That round count and the quality of the passphrase are the floor of
the design in that case.
With a threshold above 1, the verifier yields the passphrase alone.
.Pp
The loss case: the attacker holds the disk plus the records of
.Ar k
oracles, with their static keys.
That yields full offline loss of the entries that those records
cover.
The attacker searches the passphrase against one record hash, unmasks
.Ar k
shares of each entry, and reconstructs every entry key with no live
oracle.
.Pp
The middle case: the attacker holds the disk, the passphrase, and the
records of fewer than
.Ar k
oracles.
That attacker must query the remaining live oracles, online and
logged.
With
.Ar j
breached oracles, each entry costs
.Ar k No \- Ar j
requests at live oracles.
.Pp
A breached record of a retired or replaced position counts toward
the
.Ar k
breaches until the owner destroys it.
Such a record is still a passphrase verifier.
The revocation section below names the paths that destroy it.
.Ss What the oracle does not do
No oracle operation freezes a record, delays a reveal, or raises an alarm.
No oracle operation limits a caller beyond the three strikes of one record.
The oracle answers each request that passes its checks, and it counts
a wrong pin against one record.
The three strikes of one record are the one limit, and the oracle
applies nothing across records.
Revocation is the one path that closes the window, and the revocation
section below gives it.
The design gives no protection against a person who forces the owner
to unlock the vault.
.Ss Detection
The detection story rests on the log of the oracle, and on nothing
else.
The oracle writes one log line per request, with the outcome class of
that request, and it writes a prominent line for each wipe.
The outcome classes are ok_set, ok_get, ok_live, junk, reject, and
error.
A log line names no record at the default level, and this page
promises no attribution of a line to a record.
.Pp
Detection is manual.
No alert exists, and the oracle sends nothing.
Read the logs of the oracles, or revocation never happens.
.Pp
The signals are the request count and the wipe lines.
Each entry reveal is one request at each of the
.Ar k
oracles of the session quorum.
Each session adds one canary request at each of them.
The daily log volume of a quorum oracle therefore tracks the reveal
volume of the machines that use it.
An oracle outside the session quorum sees nothing.
.Pp
Malware that reveals a few entries per day looks like the owner in
the logs.
The attacker with the disk and the passphrase burns no strike.
It leaves one line per entry per quorum oracle, the same as the
owner.
The logs show a volume, and the volume is the one signal.
.Pp
That attacker selects its own quorum, so one unread oracle can hide
a whole quorum.
The section on the log coverage below gives the count of oracles
whose logs cover every possible quorum.
.Ss The provisioning of each oracle
The oracle of each position is one instance of any oracle that
speaks protocol v2, and FuguOracle is the reference deployment.
The operator of a FuguOracle instance runs it with
.Xr fuguoracle 8 .
Do these steps for each position of the set:
.Bl -enum
.It
Get the two values of the oracle from its operator.
They are the URL, and the static public key as the hex string that
.Xr fuguoracle-keygen 8
prints.
The two values are sufficient for the FuguOracle deployment, because
.Xr acme-client 1
supplies its certificate.
An oracle behind a private CA needs a third value, its root
certificate.
The client reads the trust anchors of
.Pa /etc/ssl/cert.pem
alone, so the root certificate must stand in that file on each
machine.
.It
Check that the URL ends with no slash.
The client appends /get_pin and /set_pin to it.
.It
Give the two values to
.Cm create
on the first machine, and to
.Cm provision
on every other machine.
Use the same order and the same threshold on every machine.
The order gives each oracle its position, and a position never
changes its index.
.It
Keep the operator of each oracle reachable.
The operator paths of a revocation need that operator.
.El
.Pp
The client needs no TLS, because the envelope of each request
authenticates the oracle.
TLS at the oracle mitigates three transport risks: traffic metadata,
availability, and envelope replay.
Without TLS, a network observer sees which machine talks to which
oracle and when.
A network attacker drops or delays the answers.
The oracle rejects a replayed envelope by its counter, and only an
envelope captured before a re-enrollment replays.
.Xr fugupass 1
states the bound of that replay.
Run each oracle behind TLS.
.Ss The 2-of-3 example
The example set is three oracles and the threshold 2.
Each machine holds one record per slot at each of the three oracles,
and one canary at each.
A reveal takes any two reachable oracles, in the order of the config
file, so the loss of one oracle host pauses nothing.
One breached oracle plus one disk is the verifier case, and two
breached oracles plus one disk is the loss case.
A revocation of one machine needs locks at two oracles, and logs at
two oracles cover every quorum.
Put the three oracles under three operators, on three hosts, and in
three places.
One host that runs two of them is one stolen disk from the loss
case.
.Ss The log reading at each oracle
The oracle logs with
.Xr syslog 3
at the facility LOG_DAEMON, under the name fuguoracle.
The default
.Xr syslog.conf 5
of
.Ox
writes that facility to
.Pa /var/log/daemon ,
and the operator reads it on the host of the oracle.
Do these steps at each oracle, each day:
.Bl -enum
.It
Count the lines of the class ok_get since the last reading.
Each entry reveal of a session that used this oracle is one such
line, and each session adds one for the canary.
Compare the count with the reveals that you made.
.It
Count the lines of the class junk.
A junk line is a wrong passphrase, a missing record, a corrupt
record, or a counter at or below the stored one.
The line names none of them.
Your own typos give junk lines, and a burst that you did not make is
a guess attack.
.It
Look for a wipe line.
A wipe line marks a record that took its third strike, and the oracle
unlinked it.
A wipe that you did not cause is an attack in progress against the
machine that holds that record.
.It
Look for lines of the class ok_set that you did not make.
Each enrollment of a ceremony gives one, and each replacement of a
revocation gives one.
.El
.Pp
A junk answer at your own machine names no cause.
Before you conclude a wipe from it, check the clock of the machine,
the counters file, and the passphrase.
Then read the log of that oracle.
A wipe stands there as a wipe line, and a low counter stands there as
a junk line.
.Pp
A count that exceeds your own reveals is the one sign of malware on a
machine, and a small excess looks like you.
A revocation destroys no data, so revoke a machine on a doubt.
.Ss The log coverage of every quorum
The attacker with the disk and the passphrase selects any
.Ar k
reachable oracles as its quorum.
Guaranteed log coverage therefore needs logs at enough live oracles
that every possible quorum intersects them: at most
.Ar k No \- 1
live oracles stay unread.
With the full set of
.Ar n
live oracles, the count is
.Ar n No \- Ar k No + 1
oracles.
.Pp
In the 2-of-3 example, three oracles are live and the threshold is
2.
The count is therefore 3 \- 2 + 1 = 2 oracles.
Logs at two of the three leave one oracle unread, and every quorum of
two holds one read oracle at least.
Logs at one oracle alone leave two unread, and the attacker takes
those two as its quorum and appears in no read log.
.Pp
Read the logs of
.Ar n No \- Ar k No + 1
oracles at least.
A set with one operator per oracle needs the reading of that many
operators, so keep that many of them reachable.
.Ss The revocation
A revocation denies a lost or compromised machine every quorum.
Run
.Cm revoke
of
.Xr fugupass 1
on a surviving machine, from the plate.
It locks the records of that machine at each chosen oracle: one wrong
attempt per record, at the highest counter value.
Lock at
.Ar n No \- Ar k No + 1
live oracles at least.
At most
.Ar k No \- 1
then stay unlocked, and no quorum forms.
In the 2-of-3 example, lock at two of the three.
A lock that is the first or the second strike of a record keeps
its file at the oracle.
No request of that record passes again.
A lock that is the third strike wipes the record, and the oracle
then accepts an enrollment of it.
The retired mark of the machine name in the registry keeps this
vault from that enrollment.
.Pp
Two operator paths exist beside the lock, and each needs a
self-hosted or cooperative operator.
The operator can stop the service, and a stop is reversible and
affects every machine.
The operator can delete the record files that the revocation kit
names, and no oracle operation reverses a deletion.
Derive the kit with
.Cm kit
of
.Xr fugupass 1
at the time of the revocation.
A saved copy of the kit is a snapshot: it names no record of a later
refill, so derive the kit again before a revocation.
.Pp
A lock retires the machine name at that oracle, and a replacement
machine provisions under a new name.
After the revocation, change the passphrase on every other machine
with
.Cm passwd ,
because the lost machine can have leaked it.
.Pp
A restore of the records directory of an oracle from a filesystem
backup rewinds every record of that oracle to the backup time.
A restore from a backup made before a revocation revives the records
that the revocation locked or deleted there.
Each revived record returns with its old counter and its old strike
count.
The operator restores the records directory only after an
incident review, and the owner then revokes the machine again at
that oracle.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fugupass 1 ,
.Xr bcrypt_pbkdf 3 ,
.Xr syslog 3 ,
